From 088cfee8a41ca40d8600d5f603fbc6a30172a6e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 18:42:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build the checker for item 1-1's capture layout, and find two
 obstacles
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 1-1's remaining half is blocked on one stated thing: a captured name's
index in the enclosing lambda's Box[] is decided by LambdaRewriter from the
tree, and a deferred body has no tree, so the layout must come from source
alone. 0101 built the free-name walk that would derive it. This builds the
checker that says whether such a derivation is correct.

The property that matters is asymmetric. Over-approximating costs a box per
creation site and the enclosing function's numeric tier for that name;
under-approximating means a deferred body resolves a name to a box that is not
there, which is a miscompile. So a single missed capture disqualifies the
approach, and the checker exists to make a zero mean something. Nine fixtures,
including one that deliberately records an empty prediction against a non-empty
truth and asserts the miss is reported — without it the rest are vacuous.

Two obstacles the item's statement does not name.

ClosureRepository holds two populations, and "which variables this lambda
captures" means one of them: bindings handed in from an enclosing scope (Setup,
appended to Inputs, index >= 0), and the lambda's own locals that something
nested captures (Convert, index == -1). A free-name walk answers the first and
correctly does not name the second. Compared against the whole repository,
`function outer(){ var q = 1; var inner = function(){ return q; }; }` reports
outer missing q — a function missing its own local. The prediction was right and
the comparison was wrong. So the deferral needs two derivations: the enclosing
function learns which of its own locals to box from its children's free-name
sets, and the deferred body learns its own free names' indices.
FreeNameScan.ForProgram already computes bottom-up and can serve both.

And every function is handed a ScriptInfo_* binding — script metadata the
compiler threads in — that no source identifier names, alongside this, arguments
and new.target. The layout needs a reserved region for compiler-introduced
captures beside the source-derived one.

What is not established, and is left that way. With the comparison corrected the
corpus reports 170 missed sites of 5 762 and 6 138 missed names, but the same
run reports Mandreel at 2 622 exact against 1 476 predicted sites — more checks
than predictions — so the per-site figures double-count somewhere. A miss rate
from an instrument that disagrees with itself is not a finding, so no go/no-go
is claimed and the item is not declared blocked on it. Next: the double-count,
then the three unexplained sample shapes, in that order.

Off by default (BROILER_JS_CAPTURE_LAYOUT_CHECK=1); changes no emission on any
setting. Compiler suite green: 1 250 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LqMsVZEFfMkp52dejVuRif
